        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexSuggestUsingNamedCred",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Auth",
    "title" : "Suggest Using Named Credentials for Authentication",
    "description" : "Detects hardcoded credentials in Apex code and suggests using Named Credentials for authentication instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nDetects hardcoded credentials used in requests to an endpoint.\n\nYou should refrain from hardcoding credentials:\n  * They are hard to mantain by being mixed in application code\n  * Particularly hard to update them when used from different classes\n  * Granting a developer access to the codebase means granting knowledge\n     of credentials, keeping a two-level access is not possible.\n  * Using different credentials for different environments is troublesome\n     and error-prone.\n\nInstead, you should use *Named Credentials* and a callout endpoint.\n\nFor more information, you can check [this](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexcode.meta/apexcode/apex_callouts_named_credentials.htm)\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void foo(String username, String password) {\n        Blob headerValue = Blob.valueOf(username + ':' + password);\n        String authorizationHeader = 'BASIC ' + EncodingUtil.base64Encode(headerValue);\n        req.setHeader('Authorization', authorizationHeader);\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexXSSFromEscapeFalse",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Enforce Escaping of Strings in Apex addError Messages",
    "description" : "This rule detects calls to addError in Apex code where string escaping is disabled, which can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nReports on calls to `addError` with disabled escaping. The message passed to `addError`\nwill be displayed directly to the user in the UI, making it prime ground for XSS\nattacks if unescaped.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    Trigger.new[0].addError(vulnerableHTMLGoesHere, false);\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Apex"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_apex_security_ApexXSSFromURLParam",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Escape or Sanitize Strings Obtained from URL Parameters in Apex",
    "description" : "Ensures that strings retrieved from URL parameters in Apex classes are properly escaped or sanitized to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.3\n\nMakes sure that all values obtained from URL parameters are properly escaped / sanitized\nto avoid XSS attacks.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic without sharing class Foo {\n    String unescapedstring = ApexPage.getCurrentPage().getParameters.get('url_param');\n    String usedLater = unescapedstring;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
