    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_ConsistentReturn",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Return Statements",
    "description" : "Ensures that functions do not mix return statements with and without a value, maintaining consistency in return usage.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nECMAScript does provide for return types on functions, and therefore there is no solid rule as to their usage.\nHowever, when a function does use returns they should all have a value, or all with no value.  Mixed return\nusage is likely a bug, or at best poor style.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfunction foo() {\n    if (condition1) {\n        return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\n// Bad\nfunction bar() {\n    if (condition1) {\n        return;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_GlobalVariable",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Global Variables",
    "description" : "This rule detects and discourages the use of undeclared global variables in JavaScript/ECMAScript code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nThis rule helps to avoid using accidently global variables by simply missing the &quot;var&quot; declaration.\nGlobal variables can lead to side-effects that are hard to debug.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nfunction(arg) {\n    notDeclaredVariable = 1;    // this will create a global variable and trigger the rule\n\n    var someVar = 1;            // this is a local variable, that's ok\n\n    window.otherGlobal = 2;     // this will not trigger the rule, although it is a global variable.\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_ScopeForInVariable",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Explicit Scoping of For-In Loop Variables",
    "description" : "Ensures that variables used in for-in loops are explicitly declared with 'var' to prevent unintended variable scope pollution.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nA for-in loop in which the variable name is not explicitly scoped to the enclosing scope with the 'var' keyword can\nrefer to a variable in an enclosing scope outside the nearest enclosing scope.  This will overwrite the\nexisting value of the variable in the outer scope when the body of the for-in is evaluated.  When the for-in loop\nhas finished, the variable will contain the last value used in the for-in, and the original value from before\nthe for-in loop will be gone.  Since the for-in variable name is most likely intended to be a temporary name, it\nis better to explicitly scope the variable name to the nearest enclosing scope with 'var'.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfunction foo() {\n    var p = 'clean';\n    function() {\n        var obj = { dirty: 'dirty' };\n        for (var p in obj) { // Use 'var' here.\n            obj[p] = obj[p];\n        }\n        return x;\n    }();\n\n    // 'p' still has value of 'clean'.\n}\n// Bad\nfunction bar() {\n    var p = 'clean';\n    function() {\n        var obj = { dirty: 'dirty' };\n        for (p in obj) { // Oh no, missing 'var' here!\n            obj[p] = obj[p];\n        }\n        return x;\n    }();\n\n    // 'p' is trashed and has value of 'dirty'!\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_bestpractices_UseBaseWithParseInt",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Providing Base with parseInt",
    "description" : "Ensures that the parseInt() function is always called with a specified base (radix) parameter.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0.1\n\nThis rule checks for usages of parseInt. While the second parameter is optional and usually defaults\nto 10 (base/radix is 10 for a decimal number), different implementations may behave differently.\nIt also improves readability, if the base is given.\n\nSee also: [parseInt()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/parseInt)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nparseInt(\"010\");    // unclear, could be interpreted as 10 or 7 (with a base of 7)\n\nparseInt(\"10\", 10); // good\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_AssignmentInOperand",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Assignment In Operand",
    "description" : "This rule detects and discourages the use of assignment expressions within operands, such as conditions in if statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid assignments in operands; this can make code more complicated and harder to read.  This is sometime\nindicative of the bug where the assignment operator '=' was used instead of the equality operator '=='.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nvar x = 2;\n// Bad\nif ((x = getX()) == 3) {\n    alert('3!');\n}\n\nfunction getX() {\n    return 3;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allowFor",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of a for statement"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowIf",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of an if statement"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowIncrementDecrement",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow increment or decrement operators within the conditional expression of an if, for, or while statement"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowTernary",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of a ternary operator"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowTernaryResults",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the result expressions of a ternary operator"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowWhile",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow assignment within the conditional expression of a while statement"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_ForLoopsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce For Loops to Use Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that all 'for' loops use curly braces to define their block statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using 'for' statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfor (var i = 0; i < 42; i++) {\n    foo();\n}\n\n// Bad\nfor (var i = 0; i < 42; i++)\n    foo();\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_IfElseStmtsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce If-Else Statements to Use Curly Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of curly braces in if-else statements to improve code clarity and prevent errors.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using if..else statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nif (foo) {\n    x++;\n} else {\n    y++;\n}\n\n// Bad\nif (foo)\n    x++;\nelse\n    y++;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_IfStmtsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce If Statements to Use Curly Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that all if statements use curly braces to define their blocks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using if statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nif (foo) {\n    x++;\n}\n\n// Bad\nif (foo)\n    x++;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_NoElseReturn",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Else After Return",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that an else block following an if block that ends with a return statement is unnecessary and should be removed.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.0\n\nThe else block in a if-else-construct is unnecessary if the `if` block contains a return.\nThen the content of the else block can be put outside.\n\nSee also: &lt;http://eslint.org/docs/rules/no-else-return&gt;\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Bad:\nif (x) {\n    return y;\n} else {\n    return z;\n}\n\n// Good:\nif (x) {\n    return y;\n}\nreturn z;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_UnnecessaryBlock",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Block",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages the use of unnecessary blocks in ECMAScript code that do not create new variable scopes and can be misleading.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAn unnecessary Block is present.  Such Blocks are often used in other languages to\nintroduce a new variable scope.  Blocks do not behave like this in ECMAScipt, and using them can\nbe misleading.  Considering removing this unnecessary Block.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nif (foo) {\n    // Ok\n}\nif (bar) {\n    {\n        // Bad\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_UnnecessaryParentheses",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Parentheses",
    "description" : "This rule detects and flags unnecessary parentheses in code expressions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nUnnecessary parentheses should be removed.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nvar x = 1; // Ok\nvar y = (1 + 1); // Ok\nvar z = ((1 + 1)); // Bad\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_UnreachableCode",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unreachable Code After Return, Break, Continue, or Throw",
    "description" : "Detects statements that appear after a return, break, continue, or throw statement within the same block, which are unreachable and thus either bugs or poor style.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nA 'return', 'break', 'continue', or 'throw' statement should be the last in a block. Statements after these\nwill never execute.  This is a bug, or extremely poor style.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nfunction foo() {\n   return 1;\n}\n// Bad\nfunction bar() {\n   var x = 1;\n   return x;\n   x = 2;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_codestyle_WhileLoopsMustUseBraces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce While Loops to Use Braces",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that while loops must always use curly braces to enclose their body statements.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using 'while' statements without using curly braces.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nwhile (true) {\n    x++;\n}\n\n// Bad\nwhile (true)\n    x++;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_errorprone_AvoidTrailingComma",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Trailing Comma in Object and Array Literals",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding trailing commas in object or array literals in JavaScript/ECMAScript code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThis rule helps improve code portability due to differences in browser treatment of trailing commas in object or array literals.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nfunction(arg) {\n    var obj1 = { a : 1 };   // Ok\n    var arr1 = [ 1, 2 ];    // Ok\n\n    var obj2 = { a : 1, };  // Syntax error in some browsers!\n    var arr2 = [ 1, 2, ];   // Length 2 or 3 depending on the browser!\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allowArrayLiteral",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow a trailing comma within an array literal"
      },
      {
        "name" : "allowObjectLiteral",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow a trailing comma within an object literal"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_errorprone_EqualComparison",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Strict Equality Comparison",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of strict equality operators (=== and !==) instead of abstract equality operators (== and !=) when comparing with true, false, or numbers.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nUsing == in condition may lead to unexpected results, as the variables are automatically casted to be of the\nsame type. The === operator avoids the casting.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// Ok\nif (someVar === true) {\n  ...\n}\n// Ok\nif (someVar !== 3) {\n  ...\n}\n// Bad\nif (someVar == true) {\n  ...\n}\n// Bad\nif (someVar != 3) {\n  ...\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_errorprone_InaccurateNumericLiteral",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Inaccurate Numeric Literals",
    "description" : "This rule detects numeric literals in JavaScript that will have a different value at runtime due to exceeding floating point precision limits.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nThe numeric literal will have a different value at runtime, which can happen if you provide too much\nprecision in a floating point number.  This may result in numeric calculations being in error.\n\n[Numbers](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number) in JavaScript\nare represented by 64bit double-precision floating point numbers internally and that's why there are some limits\nto the available precision of the number.\nSee [Number.isSafeInteger()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/isSafeInteger)\nand [Number.EPSILON](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/EPSILON).\n\nNote: This rule was named InnaccurateNumericLiteral before PMD 7.4.0.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nvar a = 9; // Ok\nvar b = 999999999999999; // Ok\nvar c = 999999999999999999999; // Not good\nvar w = 1.12e-4; // Ok\nvar x = 1.12; // Ok\nvar y = 1.1234567890123; // Ok\nvar z = 1.12345678901234567; // Not good\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_ecmascript_performance_AvoidConsoleStatements",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Console Statements",
    "description" : "This rule flags usage of console statements as they can negatively impact performance and potentially expose sensitive data.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.5.0\n\nUsing the console for logging in production might negatively impact performance.\nIn addition, logging could expose sensitive data.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nvar myObj = getData();\nconsole.log(myObj); // bad\nconsole.debug(\"myObj:\", myObj); // bad\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Javascript"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AbstractClassWithoutAbstractMethod",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Abstract Classes Without Abstract Methods",
    "description" : "Detects abstract classes that do not contain any abstract methods.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nThe abstract class does not contain any abstract methods. An abstract class suggests\nan incomplete implementation, which is to be completed by subclasses implementing the\nabstract methods. If the class is intended to be used as a base class only (not to be instantiated\ndirectly) a protected constructor can be provided to prevent direct instantiation.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic abstract class Foo {\n  void int method1() { ... }\n  void int method2() { ... }\n  // consider using abstract methods or removing\n  // the abstract modifier and adding protected constructors\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AccessorClassGeneration",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Instantiation Through Private Constructors from Outside the Class",
    "description" : "This rule detects and discourages instantiation of classes via private constructors from outside their own class, which causes generation of accessor classes in Java versions 10 and below.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.04\n\nInstantiation by way of private constructors from outside the constructor's class often causes the\ngeneration of an accessor. A factory method, or non-privatization of the constructor can eliminate this\nsituation. The generated class file is actually an interface.  It gives the accessing class the ability\nto invoke a new hidden package scope constructor that takes the interface as a supplementary parameter.\nThis turns a private constructor effectively into one with package scope, and is challenging to discern.\n\n_Note:_ This rule is only executed for Java 10 or lower.\nSince Java 11, [JEP 181: Nest-Based Access Control](https://openjdk.org/jeps/181) has been implemented. This\nmeans that in Java 11 and above accessor classes are not generated anymore.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Outer {\n void method(){\n  Inner ic = new Inner();//Causes generation of accessor class\n }\n public class Inner {\n  private Inner(){}\n }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AccessorMethodGeneration",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Prefer Package-Private Access to Avoid Synthetic Accessor Methods",
    "description" : "This rule suggests changing private fields or methods to package-private visibility to avoid synthetic accessor methods generated by the Java compiler when accessed from another class.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.4\n\nWhen accessing private fields / methods from another class, the Java compiler will generate accessor methods\nwith package-private visibility. This adds overhead, and to the dex method count on Android. This situation can\nbe avoided by changing the visibility of the field / method from private to package-private.\n\n\n_Note:_ This rule is only executed for Java 10 or lower.\nSince Java 11, [JEP 181: Nest-Based Access Control](https://openjdk.org/jeps/181) has been implemented. This\nmeans that in Java 11 and above accessor classes are not generated anymore.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class OuterClass {\n    private int counter;\n    /* package */ int id;\n\n    public class InnerClass {\n        InnerClass() {\n            OuterClass.this.counter++; // wrong accessor method will be generated\n        }\n\n        public int getOuterClassId() {\n            return OuterClass.this.id; // id is package-private, no accessor method needed\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_ArrayIsStoredDirectly",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Storing User-Supplied Arrays Directly",
    "description" : "This rule detects when user-supplied arrays are stored directly without making a defensive copy.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 2.2\n\nConstructors and methods receiving arrays should clone objects and store the copy.\nThis prevents future changes from the user from affecting the original array.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    private String [] x;\n        public void foo (String [] param) {\n        // Don't do this, make a copy of the array at least\n        this.x=param;\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidMessageDigestField",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Declaring MessageDigest as Field",
    "description" : "This rule discourages declaring a MessageDigest instance as a field in a class to prevent unsynchronized access issues.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.18.0\n\nDeclaring a MessageDigest instance as a field make this instance directly available to multiple threads.\n            Such sharing of MessageDigest instances should be avoided if possible since it leads to wrong results\n            if the access is not synchronized correctly.\n            Just create a new instance and use it locally, where you need it.\n            Creating a new instance is easier than synchronizing access to a shared instance.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport java.security.MessageDigest;\npublic class AvoidMessageDigestFieldExample {\n    private final MessageDigest sharedMd;\n    public AvoidMessageDigestFieldExample() throws Exception {\n        sharedMd = MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\");\n    }\n    public byte[] calculateHashShared(byte[] data) {\n        // sharing a MessageDigest like this without synchronizing access\n        // might lead to wrong results\n        sharedMd.reset();\n        sharedMd.update(data);\n        return sharedMd.digest();\n    }\n\n    // better\n    public byte[] calculateHash(byte[] data) throws Exception {\n        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\");\n        md.update(data);\n        return md.digest();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidPrintStackTrace",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid printStackTrace()",
    "description" : "This rule flags the usage of printStackTrace() calls and recommends using a logger instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.2\n\nAvoid printStackTrace(); use a logger call instead.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Foo {\n    void bar() {\n        try {\n            // do something\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidReassigningCatchVariables",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Reassigning Catch Variables",
    "description" : "This rule disallows reassigning the exception variable caught in a catch statement.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.27.0\n\nReassigning exception variables caught in a catch statement should be avoided because of:\n\n1) If it is needed, multi catch can be easily added and code will still compile.\n\n2) Following the principle of least surprise we want to make sure that a variable caught in a catch statement\nis always the one thrown in a try block.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    public void foo() {\n        try {\n            // do something\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            e = new NullPointerException(); // not recommended\n        }\n\n        try {\n            // do something\n        } catch (MyException | ServerException e) {\n            e = new RuntimeException(); // won't compile\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidReassigningLoopVariables",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Reassigning Loop Variables",
    "description" : "This rule detects and prevents the reassignment of loop control variables within the loop body.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.11.0\n\nReassigning loop variables can lead to hard-to-find bugs. Prevent or limit how these variables can be changed.\n\nIn foreach-loops, configured by the `foreachReassign` property:\n- `deny`: Report any reassignment of the loop variable in the loop body. _This is the default._\n- `allow`: Don't check the loop variable.\n- `firstOnly`: Report any reassignments of the loop variable, except as the first statement in the loop body.\n            _This is useful if some kind of normalization or clean-up of the value before using is permitted, but any other change of the variable is not._\n\nIn for-loops, configured by the `forReassign` property:\n- `deny`: Report any reassignment of the control variable in the loop body. _This is the default._\n- `allow`: Don't check the control variable.\n- `skip`: Report any reassignments of the control variable, except conditional increments/decrements (`++`, `--`, `+=`, `-=`).\n            _This prevents accidental reassignments or unconditional increments of the control variable._\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n  private void foo() {\n    for (String s : listOfStrings()) {\n      s = s.trim(); // OK, when foreachReassign is \"firstOnly\" or \"allow\"\n      doSomethingWith(s);\n\n      s = s.toUpper(); // OK, when foreachReassign is \"allow\"\n      doSomethingElseWith(s);\n    }\n\n    for (int i=0; i < 10; i++) {\n      if (check(i)) {\n        i++; // OK, when forReassign is \"skip\" or \"allow\"\n      }\n\n      i = 5;  // OK, when forReassign is \"allow\"\n\n      doSomethingWith(i);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidReassigningParameters",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Reassigning Parameters",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding reassignment of method or constructor parameters.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 1.0\n\nReassigning values to incoming parameters of a method or constructor is not recommended, as this can\nmake the code more difficult to understand. The code is often read with the assumption that parameter values\ndon't change and an assignment violates therefore the principle of least astonishment. This is especially a\nproblem if the parameter is documented e.g. in the method's javadoc and the new content differs from the original\ndocumented content.\n\nUse temporary local variables instead. This allows you to assign a new name, which makes the code better\nunderstandable.\n\nNote that this rule considers both methods and constructors. If there are multiple assignments for a formal\nparameter, then only the first assignment is reported.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Hello {\n  private void greet(String name) {\n    name = name.trim();\n    System.out.println(\"Hello \" + name);\n\n    // preferred\n    String trimmedName = name.trim();\n    System.out.println(\"Hello \" + trimmedName);\n  }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidStringBufferField",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid StringBuffer Field",
    "description" : "Detects instance fields of type StringBuffer which can cause memory leaks if the owning object has a long lifetime.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nStringBuffers/StringBuilders can grow considerably, and so may become a source of memory leaks\nif held within objects with long lifetimes.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    private StringBuffer buffer;    // potential memory leak as an instance variable;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_AvoidUsingHardCodedIP",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Hard Coded IP Addresses",
    "description" : "This rule flags the use of hard-coded IP addresses in the source code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.1\n\nApplication with hard-coded IP addresses can become impossible to deploy in some cases.\nExternalizing IP adresses is preferable.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    private String ip = \"127.0.0.1\";     // not recommended\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_CheckResultSet",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Checking ResultSet Navigation Method Returns",
    "description" : "Ensure that the return values of ResultSet navigation methods (next, previous, first, last) are always checked before accessing data.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.1\n\nAlways check the return values of navigation methods (next, previous, first, last) of a ResultSet.\nIf the value return is 'false', it should be handled properly.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nStatement stat = conn.createStatement();\nResultSet rst = stat.executeQuery(\"SELECT name FROM person\");\nrst.next();     // what if it returns false? bad form\nString firstName = rst.getString(1);\n\nStatement stat = conn.createStatement();\nResultSet rst = stat.executeQuery(\"SELECT name FROM person\");\nif (rst.next()) {    // result is properly examined and used\n    String firstName = rst.getString(1);\n    } else  {\n        // handle missing data\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_bestpractices_ConstantsInInterface",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Constants in Interfaces",
    "description" : "This rule discourages defining constants in interfaces, as interfaces should define types, not hold implementation details like constants.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5\n\nUsing constants in interfaces is a bad practice. Interfaces define types, constants are implementation details better placed in classes or enums. If the constants are best viewed as members of an enumerated type, you should export them with an enum type.\nFor other scenarios, consider using a utility class. See Effective Java's 'Use interfaces only to define types'.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic interface ConstantInterface {\n    public static final int CONST1 = 1; // violation, no fields allowed in interface!\n    static final int CONST2 = 1;        // violation, no fields allowed in interface!\n    final int CONST3 = 1;               // violation, no fields allowed in interface!\n    int CONST4 = 1;                     // violation, no fields allowed in interface!\n}\n\n// with ignoreIfHasMethods = false\npublic interface AnotherConstantInterface {\n    public static final int CONST1 = 1; // violation, no fields allowed in interface!\n\n    int anyMethod();\n}\n\n// with ignoreIfHasMethods = true\npublic interface YetAnotherConstantInterface {\n    public static final int CONST1 = 1; // no violation\n\n    int anyMethod();\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreIfHasMethods",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Whether to ignore constants in interfaces if the interface defines any methods"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
