      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_TooFewBranchesForSwitch",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Switch Statements With Too Few Branches",
    "description" : "Detects switch statements with fewer than three branches and suggests using if-else statements instead.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.2\n\nSwitch statements are intended to be used to support complex branching behaviour. Using a switch for only a few\ncases is ill-advised, since switches are not as easy to understand as if-else statements. In these cases use the\nif-else statement to increase code readability.\n\nNote: This rule was named TooFewBranchesForASwitchStatement before PMD 7.7.0.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n// With a minimumNumberCaseForASwitch of 3\npublic class Foo {\n    public void bar(int condition) {\n        switch (condition) {\n            case 1:\n                instruction;\n                break;\n            default:\n                break; // not enough for a 'switch' stmt, a simple 'if' stmt would have been more appropriate\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "minimumNumberCaseForASwitch",
        "default" : "3",
        "description" : "Minimum number of branches for a switch"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseArrayListInsteadOfVector",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use ArrayList Instead Of Vector",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of ArrayList instead of Vector for collections when thread safety is not required.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.0\n\nArrayList is a much better Collection implementation than Vector if thread-safe operation is not required.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport java.util.*;\npublic class SimpleTest extends TestCase {\n    public void testX() {\n    Collection c1 = new Vector();\n    Collection c2 = new ArrayList();    // achieves the same with much better performance\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseArraysAsList",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use Arrays.asList Instead of Manual Array Copy Loops",
    "description" : "Detects manual loops copying array elements to a list and suggests using Arrays.asList() for better performance and cleaner code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.5\n\nThe `java.util.Arrays` class has a `asList()` method that should be used when you want to create a new List from\nan array of objects. It is faster than executing a loop to copy all the elements of the array one by one.\n\nNote that the result of `Arrays.asList()` is backed by the specified array,\nchanges in the returned list will result in the array to be modified.\nFor that reason, it is not possible to add new elements to the returned list of `Arrays.asList()`\n(UnsupportedOperationException).\nYou must use `new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(Arrays.asList(...))` if that is inconvenient for you (e.g. because of concurrent access).\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Test {\n    public void foo(Integer[] ints) {\n        // could just use Arrays.asList(ints)\n        List<Integer> l = new ArrayList<>(100);\n        for (int i = 0; i < ints.length; i++) {\n            l.add(ints[i]);\n        }\n\n        List<Integer> anotherList = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < ints.length; i++) {\n            anotherList.add(ints[i].toString()); // won't trigger the rule\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseIndexOfChar",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use IndexOf(char) Instead of IndexOf(String)",
    "description" : "This rule enforces using String.indexOf(char) when searching for a single character in a string, as it performs faster than String.indexOf(String).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.5\n\nUse String.indexOf(char) when checking for the index of a single character; it executes faster.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nString s = \"hello world\";\n// avoid this\nif (s.indexOf(\"d\") {}\n// instead do this\nif (s.indexOf('d') {}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseIOStreamsWithApacheCommonsFileItem",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Memory Intensive FileItem.get() or FileItem.getString() Usage",
    "description" : "This rule detects the usage of FileItem.get() or FileItem.getString() methods which load the entire file into memory, potentially causing memory exhaustion.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.25.0\n\nProblem: Use of [FileItem.get()](https://javadoc.io/static/commons-fileupload/commons-fileupload/1.5/org/apache/commons/fileupload/FileItem.html#get--)\nand [FileItem.getString()](https://javadoc.io/static/commons-fileupload/commons-fileupload/1.5/org/apache/commons/fileupload/FileItem.html#getString--)\ncould exhaust memory since they load the entire file into memory.\n\nSolution: Use [FileItem.getInputStream()](https://javadoc.io/static/commons-fileupload/commons-fileupload/1.5/org/apache/commons/fileupload/FileItem.html#getInputStream--)\nand buffering.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nimport org.apache.commons.fileupload.FileItem;\n\npublic class FileStuff {\n   private String bad(FileItem fileItem) {\n        return fileItem.getString();\n   }\n\n   private InputStream good(FileItem fileItem) {\n        return fileItem.getInputStream();\n   }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UselessStringValueOf",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Useless String.valueOf Calls",
    "description" : "Detects unnecessary calls to String.valueOf when appending to a string, where the argument can be used directly.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.8\n\nNo need to call String.valueOf to append to a string; just use the valueOf() argument directly.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic String convert(int i) {\n    String s;\n    s = \"a\" + String.valueOf(i);    // not required\n    s = \"a\" + i;                    // preferred approach\n    return s;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseStringBufferForStringAppends",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use StringBuilder or StringBuffer for String Appends",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of StringBuilder or StringBuffer for string concatenations instead of using the '+=' operator in loops or multiple concatenations.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.1\n\nThe use of the '+=' operator for appending strings causes the JVM to create and use an internal StringBuffer.\nIf a non-trivial number of these concatenations are being used then the explicit use of a StringBuilder or\nthreadsafe StringBuffer is recommended to avoid this.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    String inefficientConcatenation() {\n        String result = \"\";\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            // warning: this concatenation will create one new StringBuilder per iteration\n            result += getStringFromSomeWhere(i);\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    String efficientConcatenation() {\n        // better would be to use one StringBuilder for the entire loop\n        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n            result.append(getStringFromSomeWhere(i));\n        }\n        return result.toString();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_performance_UseStringBufferLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use StringBuffer Length Instead of toString for Length Checks",
    "description" : "This rule enforces using StringBuffer.length() to check the length instead of converting to a String with toString() and then checking length or equality.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.4\n\nUse StringBuffer.length() to determine StringBuffer length rather than using StringBuffer.toString().equals(&quot;&quot;)\nor StringBuffer.toString().length() == ...\n\nExample(s):\n```\nStringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer();\n\nif (sb.toString().equals(\"\")) {}        // inefficient\n\nif (sb.length() == 0) {}                // preferred\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_security_HardCodedCryptoKey",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "InsecureStorage",
    "title" : "Avoid Hard Coded Crypto Keys",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of hard coded cryptographic keys in the source code, which should be avoided.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.4.0\n\nDo not use hard coded values for cryptographic operations. Please store keys outside of source code.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    void good() {\n        SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec = new SecretKeySpec(Properties.getKey(), \"AES\");\n    }\n\n    void bad() {\n        SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec = new SecretKeySpec(\"my secret here\".getBytes(), \"AES\");\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_java_security_InsecureCryptoIv",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "Cryptography",
    "title" : "Avoid Hard Coded Initialization Vector in Cryptographic Operations",
    "description" : "This rule detects the use of hard coded initialization vectors (IV) in cryptographic operations.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.3.0\n\nDo not use hard coded initialization vector in cryptographic operations. Please use a randomly generated IV.\n\nExample(s):\n```\npublic class Foo {\n    void good() {\n        SecureRandom random = new SecureRandom();\n        byte iv[] = new byte[16];\n        random.nextBytes(bytes);\n    }\n\n    void bad() {\n        byte[] iv = new byte[] { 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, 00, };\n    }\n\n    void alsoBad() {\n        byte[] iv = \"secret iv in here\".getBytes();\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_bestpractices_DontNestJsfInJstlIteration",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Nesting JSF Components Inside JSTL Iteration",
    "description" : "This rule prevents nesting JSF component custom actions inside JSTL iteration tags that iterate over their body content.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.6\n\nDo not nest JSF component custom actions inside a custom action that iterates over its body.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <ul>\n      <c:forEach items='${books}' var='b'>\n        <li> <h:outputText value='#{b}' /> </li>\n      </c:forEach>\n    </ul>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```",
