  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_design_NoInlineScript",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Inline HTML Script",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding inline HTML script content within JSP pages or HTML files.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.0\n\nAvoid inlining HTML script content.  Consider externalizing the HTML script using the 'src' attribute on the &quot;script&quot; element.\nExternalized script could be reused between pages.  Browsers can also cache the script, reducing overall download bandwidth.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_design_NoInlineStyleInformation",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Inline Style Information in JSP Files",
    "description" : "This rule prohibits the use of inline style information within JSP files, such as HTML tags like <B> or <FONT> or attributes like align='center'.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.6\n\nStyle information should be put in CSS files, not in JSPs. Therefore, don't use &lt;B&gt; or &lt;FONT&gt;\ntags, or attributes like &quot;align='center'&quot;.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<html><body><p align='center'><b>text</b></p></body></html>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_design_NoLongScripts",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Long Scripts Inside JSP Files",
    "description" : "This rule detects and discourages the inclusion of long script blocks (e.g., JavaScript) directly inside JSP files.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.6\n\nScripts should be part of Tag Libraries, rather than part of JSP pages.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<HTML>\n<BODY>\n<!--Java Script-->\n<SCRIPT language=\"JavaScript\" type=\"text/javascript\">\n<!--\nfunction calcDays(){\n  var date1 = document.getElementById('d1').lastChild.data;\n  var date2 = document.getElementById('d2').lastChild.data;\n  date1 = date1.split(\"-\");\n  date2 = date2.split(\"-\");\n  var sDate = new Date(date1[0]+\"/\"+date1[1]+\"/\"+date1[2]);\n  var eDate = new Date(date2[0]+\"/\"+date2[1]+\"/\"+date2[2]);\n  var daysApart = Math.abs(Math.round((sDate-eDate)/86400000));\n  document.getElementById('diffDays').lastChild.data = daysApart;\n}\n\nonload=calcDays;\n//-->\n</SCRIPT>\n</BODY>\n</HTML>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_design_NoScriptlets",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Scriptlets in JSP Files",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding the use of scriptlets inside JSP files.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.6\n\nScriptlets should be factored into Tag Libraries or JSP declarations, rather than being part of JSP pages.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<HTML>\n<HEAD>\n<%\nresponse.setHeader(\"Pragma\", \"No-cache\");\n%>\n</HEAD>\n    <BODY>\n        <jsp:scriptlet>String title = \"Hello world!\";</jsp:scriptlet>\n    </BODY>\n</HTML>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_errorprone_JspEncoding",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce UTF-8 Encoding in JSP Files",
    "description" : "Ensures that JSP files specify UTF-8 encoding to maintain consistent character encoding.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 4.0\n\nA missing 'meta' tag or page directive will trigger this rule, as well as a non-UTF-8 charset.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nMost browsers should be able to interpret the following headers:\n\n<%@ page contentType=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\" pageEncoding=\"UTF-8\" %>\n\n<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Type\"  content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\" />\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_security_IframeMissingSrcAttribute",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "UnexpectedBehaviour",
    "title" : "Enforce IFrames to Have Src Attribute",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that all iframe elements include a src attribute.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 3.6\n\nIFrames which are missing a src element can cause security information popups in IE if you are accessing the page\nthrough SSL. See http://support.microsoft.com/default.aspx?scid=kb;EN-US;Q261188\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<HTML><title>bad example><BODY>\n<iframe></iframe>\n</BODY> </HTML>\n\n<HTML><title>good example><BODY>\n<iframe src=\"foo\"></iframe>\n</BODY> </HTML>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_jsp_security_NoUnsanitizedJSPExpression",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Avoid Unsanitized JSP Expression",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of JSP expressions that are not properly escaped or sanitized, which can lead to Cross Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1.4\n\nAvoid using expressions without escaping / sanitizing. This could lead to cross site scripting - as the expression\nwould be interpreted by the browser directly (e.g. &quot;&lt;script&gt;alert('hello');&lt;/script&gt;&quot;).\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<%@ page contentType=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\" %>\n<%@ taglib prefix=\"fn\" uri=\"http://java.sun.com/jsp/jstl/functions\" %>\n${expression}                    <!-- don't use this -->\n${fn:escapeXml(expression)}      <!-- instead, escape it -->\n<c:out value=\"${expression}\" />  <!-- or use c:out -->\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "JSP"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_kotlin_bestpractices_FunctionNameTooShort",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Function Names That Are Too Short",
    "description" : "Function names should be clear and descriptive rather than cryptic or too short.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nFunction names should be easy to understand and describe the intention. Makes developers happy.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nfun cl() {} // violation, no unavailable attribute added to the function declaration\n\nfun calculateLayout() // no violation\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Kotlin"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_kotlin_errorprone_OverrideBothEqualsAndHashcode",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Override Both Equals And Hashcode",
    "description" : "Ensure that both equals() and hashCode() methods are overridden together in a class.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nOverride both public boolean Object.equals(Object other), and public int Object.hashCode(), or override neither.  Even if you are inheriting a hashCode() from a parent class, consider implementing hashCode and explicitly delegating to your superclass.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass Bar {        // poor, missing a hashCode() method\n    override fun equals(o: Any?): Boolean {\n      // do some comparison\n    }\n}\n\nclass Baz {        // poor, missing an equals() method\n    override fun hashCode(): Int {\n      // return some hash value\n    }\n}\n\nclass Foo {        // perfect, both methods provided\n    override fun equals(other: Any?): Boolean {\n      // do some comparison\n    }\n    override fun hashCode(): Int {\n      // return some hash value\n    }\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Kotlin"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_bestpractices_TomKytesDespair",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using WHEN OTHERS THEN NULL",
    "description" : "This rule flags the use of 'WHEN OTHERS THEN NULL' in PL/SQL exception handling, which hides all errors silently.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\n&quot;WHEN OTHERS THEN NULL&quot; hides all errors - (Re)RAISE an exception or call RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY update_planned_hrs\nIS\n\nPROCEDURE set_new_planned (p_emp_id IN NUMBER, p_project_id IN NUMBER, p_hours IN NUMBER)\nIS\nBEGIN\n   UPDATE employee_on_activity ea\n   SET ea.ea_planned_hours = p_hours\n   WHERE\n            ea.ea_emp_id = p_emp_id\n            AND ea.ea_proj_id = p_project_id;\n\nEXCEPTION\n          WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN\n          RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR (-20100, 'No such employee or project');\n\nEND set_new_planned;\n\nFUNCTION existing_planned (p_emp_id IN NUMBER, p_project_id IN NUMBER) RETURN NUMBER\n\nIS\n\nexisting_hours NUMBER(4);\n\nBEGIN\n   SELECT ea.ea_planned_hours INTO existing_hours\n   FROM employee_on_activity ea\n   WHERE\n            ea.ea_emp_id = p_emp_id\n            AND ea.ea_proj_id = p_project_id;\n\n   RETURN (existing_hours);\n\n   EXCEPTION\n          WHEN OTHERS THEN NULL;\n\n   END existing_planned;\n\nEND update_planned_hrs;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_codestyle_AvoidTabCharacter",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Tab Character for Indentation",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of spaces instead of tab characters for indentation in source files.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.13.0\n\nThis rule checks, that there are no tab characters (`\\t`) in the source file.\nIt reports only the first occurrence per file.\n\nUsing tab characters for indentation is not recommended, since this requires that every developer\nuses the same tab with in their editor.\n\nThis rule is the PMD equivalent of checkstyle's [FileTabCharacter](http://checkstyle.sourceforge.net/config_whitespace.html#FileTabCharacter) check.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_codestyle_CodeFormat",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Proper Code Formatting",
    "description" : "Ensures that PLSQL code follows consistent formatting and indentation rules for readability and maintainability.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.9.0\n\nThis rule verifies that the PLSQL code is properly formatted. The following checks are executed:\n\nSQL Queries:\n\n*   The selected columns must be each on a new line\n*   The keywords (BULK COLLECT INTO, FROM) start on a new line and are indented by one level\n*   UNION should be on the same indentation level as SELECT\n*   Each JOIN is on a new line. If there are more than one JOIN conditions, then each condition should be\n    on a separate line.\n\nParameter definitions for procedures:\n\n*   Each parameter should be on a new line\n*   Variable names as well as their types should be aligned\n\nVariable declarations:\n\n*   Each variable should be on a new line\n*   Variable names as well as their types should be aligned\n\nCalling a procedure:\n\n*   If there are more than 3 parameters\n    *   then named parameters should be used\n    *   and each parameter should be on a new line\n\nExample(s):\n```\nBEGIN\n  -- select columns each on a separate line\n  SELECT cmer_id\n        ,version\n        ,cmp_id\n    BULK COLLECT INTO v_cmer_ids\n        ,v_versions\n        ,v_cmp_ids\n    FROM cmer;\n\n  -- each parameter on a new line\n  PROCEDURE create_prospect(\n    company_info_in      IN    prospects.company_info%TYPE -- Organization\n   ,firstname_in         IN    persons.firstname%TYPE      -- FirstName\n   ,lastname_in          IN    persons.lastname%TYPE       -- LastName\n  );\n\n  -- more than three parameters, each parameter on a separate line\n  webcrm_marketing.prospect_ins(\n    cmp_id_in            => NULL\n   ,company_info_in      => company_info_in\n   ,firstname_in         => firstname_in\n   ,lastname_in          => lastname_in\n   ,slt_code_in          => NULL\n  );\n\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_codestyle_ForLoopNaming",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use Meaningful Names for For Loop Variables",
    "description" : "Enforce the use of meaningful names for variables in FOR loops in PL/SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.7.0\n\nIn case you have loops please name the loop variables more meaningful.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n-- good example\nBEGIN\nFOR company IN (SELECT * FROM companies) LOOP\n  FOR contact IN (SELECT * FROM contacts) LOOP\n    FOR party IN (SELECT * FROM parties) LOOP\n      NULL;\n    END LOOP;\n  END LOOP;\nEND LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- bad example\nBEGIN\nFOR c1 IN (SELECT * FROM companies) LOOP\n  FOR c2 IN (SELECT * FROM contacts) LOOP\n    FOR c3 IN (SELECT * FROM parties) LOOP\n      NULL;\n    END LOOP;\n  END LOOP;\nEND LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allowSimpleLoops",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Ignore simple loops, that are not nested"
      },
      {
        "name" : "cursorPattern",
        "default" : "[a-zA-Z_0-9]{5,}",
        "description" : "The pattern used for the cursor loop variable"
      },
      {
        "name" : "indexPattern",
        "default" : "[a-zA-Z_0-9]{5,}",
        "description" : "The pattern used for the index loop variable"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_codestyle_LineLength",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Maximum Line Length",
    "description" : "This rule checks if any line in the code exceeds a predefined maximum length.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.13.0\n\nThis rule checks for long lines. Please note that comments are not ignored.\n\nThis rule is the PMD equivalent of checkstyle's [LineLength](http://checkstyle.sourceforge.net/config_sizes.html#LineLength) check.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_codestyle_MisplacedPragma",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Misplaced Pragma in PL/SQL",
    "description" : "Ensures that PRAGMA directives are used only inside the declaration block before the BEGIN statement in PL/SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.5.2\n\nOracle states that the PRAQMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION must be in the declaration block,\nbut the code does not complain, when being compiled on the 11g DB.\nhttps://docs.oracle.com/cd/B28359_01/appdev.111/b28370/static.htm#BABIIHBJ\n\nExample(s):\n```\ncreate or replace package inline_pragma_error is\n\nend;\n/\n\ncreate or replace package body inline_pragma_error is\n  procedure do_transaction(p_input_token        in varchar(200)) is\n  PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION; /* this is correct place for PRAGMA */\n  begin\n    PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION; /* this is the wrong place for PRAGMA -> violation */\n    /* do something */\n    COMMIT;\n   end do_transaction;\n\nend inline_pragma_error;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_CyclomaticComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Cyclomatic Complexity Limits",
    "description" : "This rule checks the cyclomatic complexity of methods or functions to ensure they are not too complex.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nComplexity directly affects maintenance costs is determined by the number of decision points in a method\nplus one for the method entry.  The decision points include 'if', 'while', 'for', and 'case labels' calls.\nGenerally, numbers ranging from 1-4 denote low complexity, 5-7 denote moderate complexity, 8-10 denote\nhigh complexity, and 11+ is very high complexity.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n-- Cyclomatic Complexity of 25\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY pkg_pmd_working_sequence  AS\n1 PROCEDURE ty_logger  IS BEGIN\n2        IF true\n         THEN\n              DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n3        IF true\n         THEN\n              DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n4            IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n5            ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n6        ELSIF false\n         THEN\n            DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n7            IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n8            ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n         ELSE\n            DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n9            IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n10           ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n         END IF;\n11         ELSIF false\n         THEN\n            DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n12       IF true\n         THEN\n              DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n13           IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n14           ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n15       ELSIF false\n         THEN\n16           IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n17           ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n             DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n         ELSE\n             DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n         END IF;\n     ELSE\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n18       IF true\n         THEN\n              DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n19           IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n20           ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n21       ELSIF false\n         THEN\n            DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n22           IF true\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n23           ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n             ELSE\n             DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n24           IF true\n             THEN\n                  DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('IF/THEN l_Integer='||l_integer);\n25           ELSIF false\n             THEN\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             ELSE\n                DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('ELSIF l_Integer='||l_integer);\n             END IF;\n         END IF;\n     END IF;\nEND;\n\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessiveMethodLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Method Length",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods that are excessively long, which can indicate that the method is doing too much and is difficult to read and maintain.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nWhen methods are excessively long this usually indicates that the method is doing more than its\nname/signature might suggest. They also become challenging for others to digest since excessive\nscrolling causes readers to lose focus.\nTry to reduce the method length by creating helper methods and removing any copy/pasted code.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE doSomething BEGIN\n    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(\"Hello world!\");\n    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(\"Hello world!\");\n        -- 98 copies omitted for brevity.\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessiveObjectLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Object Length",
    "description" : "This rule discourages creating Oracle objects (such as packages) with excessively long specifications or bodies.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nExcessive object line lengths are usually indications that the object may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by other objects. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPACKAGE BODY Foo AS\n    PROCEDURE bar1 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar1;\n    PROCEDURE bar2 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar2;\n    PROCEDURE bar3 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar3;\n\n\n    PROCEDURE barN IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END barn;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessivePackageBodyLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Package Body Length",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages very long Object Type and Package bodies in PL/SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nExcessive class file lengths are usually indications that the class may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by external classes or functions. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPACKAGE BODY Foo AS\n    PROCEDURE bar1 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar1;\n    PROCEDURE bar2 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar2;\n    PROCEDURE bar3 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar3;\n\n\n    PROCEDURE barN IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END barn;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 20,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessivePackageSpecificationLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Package Specification Length",
    "description" : "This rule checks for package specifications that are excessively long in PL/SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nExcessive class file lengths are usually indications that the class may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by external classes or functions. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPACKAGE Foo AS\n    PROCEDURE bar1;\n    PROCEDURE bar2;\n    PROCEDURE bar3;\n\n    ...\n\n    PROCEDURE barN;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessiveParameterList",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Parameter List",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods or procedures with an excessive number of parameters.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nMethods with numerous parameters are a challenge to maintain, especially if most of them share the\nsame datatype. These situations usually denote the need for new objects to wrap the numerous parameters.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE addPerson(        -- too many arguments liable to be mixed up\n    birthYear pls_integer, birthMonth pls_integer, birthDate pls_integer, height pls_integer, weight pls_integer, ssn pls_integer) {\n\n    . . . .\nEND ADDPERSON;\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE addPerson(        -- preferred approach\n    birthdate DATE, measurements BodyMeasurements , ssn INTEGER) BEGIN\n\n    . . . .\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_ExcessiveTypeLength",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Excessive Length in Object Type Specifications",
    "description" : "This rule detects when an Object Type specification in PL/SQL becomes excessively long, indicating that it may have too many responsibilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nExcessive class file lengths are usually indications that the class may be burdened with excessive\nresponsibilities that could be provided by external classes or functions. In breaking these methods\napart the code becomes more managable and ripe for reuse.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nTYPE BODY Foo AS\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE bar1 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar1;\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE bar2 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar2;\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE bar3 IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END bar3;\n\n\n    MEMBER PROCEDURE barN IS BEGIN\n    -- 1000 lines of code\n    END barn;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_NcssMethodCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit NCSS Method Count",
    "description" : "This rule checks if a method exceeds a certain number of Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) lines, which measures the actual lines of code excluding comments.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given method. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY AS\n FUNCTION methd RETURN INTEGER IS\n BEGIN\n   RETURN 1;;\n END;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_NcssObjectCount",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce NCSS Object Count Threshold",
    "description" : "This rule checks the Non-Commenting Source Statements (NCSS) line count of Oracle objects to monitor their size and complexity.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThis rule uses the NCSS (Non-Commenting Source Statements) algorithm to determine the number of lines\nof code for a given Oracle object. NCSS ignores comments, and counts actual statements. Using this algorithm,\nlines of code that are split are counted as one.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE pkg_\n PROCEDURE Foo IS\n BEGIN\n --this class only has 6 NCSS lines\n     super();\n     super();\n END;\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_NPathComplexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Limit NPath Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects methods with high NPath complexity, which counts the number of acyclic execution paths through a method.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nThe NPath complexity of a method is the number of acyclic execution paths through that method.\nA threshold of 200 is generally considered the point where measures should be taken to reduce\ncomplexity and increase readability.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE\nPROCEDURE bar AS BEGIN  -- this is something more complex than it needs to be,\n    if (y) THEN -- it should be broken down into smaller methods or functions\n        for j IN 0 .. j-1 LOOP\n            if (j > r) THEN\n                doSomething;\n                while (f < 5 ) LOOP\n                    anotherThing;\n                    f := f - 27;\n                    END LOOP;\n            else\n                tryThis();\n            END IF;\n        END LOOP;\n    END IF;\n    if ( r - n > 45) THEN\n        while (doMagic) LOOP\n            findRabbits;\n        END LOOP;\n    END IF;\n    BEGIN\n        doSomethingDangerous();\n    EXCEPTION WHEN FooException THEN\n        makeAmends;\n        BEGIN\n            dontDoItAgain;\n        EXCEPTION\n        WHEN OTHERS THEN\n            log_problem;\n        END;\n    END;\nEND;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_TooManyFields",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Too Many Fields in Classes or Packages",
    "description" : "Detects classes or packages with an excessive number of fields which can make them difficult to maintain and understand.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nClasses that have too many fields can become unwieldy and could be redesigned to have fewer fields,\npossibly through grouping related fields in new objects.  For example, a class with individual\ncity/state/zip fields could park them within a single Address field.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE pkg_too_many_fields AS\n    C_CHAR_A CONSTANT CHAR(1 CHAR) := 'A';\n    C_CHAR_B CONSTANT CHAR(1 CHAR) := 'B';\n    ...\n    C_CHAR_Z CONSTANT CHAR(1 CHAR) := 'Z';\nEND pkg_too_many_fields;\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_design_TooManyMethods",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
