    "title" : "Avoid Too Many Methods in a Single Object",
    "description" : "Detects objects with an excessive number of methods suggesting refactoring to reduce complexity.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nA package or type with too many methods is probably a good suspect for refactoring, in order to reduce its complexity and find a way to\nhave more fine grained objects.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "maxmethods",
        "default" : "1",
        "description" : "The method count reporting threshold"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_errorprone_TO_DATE_TO_CHAR",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid TO_DATE(TO_CHAR(variable)) for Removing Time Component",
    "description" : "Detects usage of TO_DATE(TO_CHAR(date-variable)) to remove the time component of a date, which should be replaced with TRUNC(date-variable).",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nTO_DATE(TO_CHAR(date-variable)) used to remove time component - use TRUNC(date-variable)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY date_utilities\nIS\n\n-- Take single parameter, relying on current default NLS date format\nFUNCTION strip_time (p_date IN DATE) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   RETURN TO_DATE(TO_CHAR(p_date));\nEND strip_time;\n\n\nEND date_utilities;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_errorprone_TO_DATEWithoutDateFormat",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid TO_DATE Without Date Format",
    "description" : "Detects usage of TO_DATE function without specifying an explicit date format string.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nTO_DATE without date format- use TO_DATE(expression, date-format)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY date_utilities\nIS\n\n-- Take single parameter, relying on current default NLS date format\nFUNCTION to_date_single_parameter (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   RETURN TO_DATE(p_date_string);\nEND to_date_single_parameter ;\n\n-- Take 2 parameters, using an explicit date format string\nFUNCTION to_date_two_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_DATE(p_date_string, p_date_format);\nEND to_date_two_parameters;\n\n-- Take 3 parameters, using an explicit date format string and an explicit language\nFUNCTION to_date_three_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2, p_nls_language VARCHAR2 ) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_DATE(p_date_string, p_format_mask, p_nls_language);\nEND to_date_three_parameters;\n\nEND date_utilities;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_plsql_errorprone_TO_TIMESTAMPWithoutDateFormat",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid TO_TIMESTAMP Without Date Format",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of the TO_TIMESTAMP function without an explicit date format string in PL/SQL code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.1\n\nTO_TIMESTAMP without date format- use TO_TIMESTAMP(expression, date-format)\n\nExample(s):\n```\nCREATE OR REPLACE PACKAGE BODY date_utilities\nIS\n\n-- Take single parameter, relying on current default NLS date format\nFUNCTION to_timestamp_single_parameter (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   RETURN TO_TIMESTAMP(p_date_string);\nEND to_timestamp_single_parameter;\n\n-- Take 2 parameters, using an explicit date format string\nFUNCTION to_timestamp_two_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_TIMESTAMP(p_date_string, p_date_format);\nEND to_timestamp_two_parameters;\n\n-- Take 3 parameters, using an explicit date format string and an explicit language\nFUNCTION to_timestamp_three_parameters (p_date_string IN VARCHAR2, p_format_mask IN VARCHAR2, p_nls_language VARCHAR2 ) RETURN DATE\nIS\nBEGIN\n   TO_TIMESTAMP(p_date_string, p_format_mask, p_nls_language);\nEND to_timestamp_three_parameters;\n\nEND date_utilities;\n/\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "PLSQL"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_pom_errorprone_InvalidDependencyTypes",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Invalid Dependency Types in Maven",
    "description" : "Detects usage of invalid dependency types in Maven's dependency management section that are ignored by Maven instead of causing a build failure.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4\n\nIf you use an invalid dependency type in the dependency management section, Maven doesn't fail. Instead,\nthe entry is just ignored, which might have the effect, that the wrong version of the dependency is used.\n\nThe following types are considered valid: pom, jar, maven-plugin, ejb, war, ear, rar, par.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<project...>\n  ...\n  <dependencyManagement>\n      ...\n    <dependency>\n      <groupId>org.jboss.arquillian</groupId>\n      <artifactId>arquillian-bom</artifactId>\n      <version>${arquillian.version}</version>\n      <type>bom</type> <!-- not a valid type ! 'pom' is ! -->\n      <scope>import</scope>\n    </dependency>\n    ...\n  </dependencyManagement>\n</project>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "validTypes",
        "default" : "pom,jar,maven-plugin,ejb,war,ear,rar,par",
        "description" : "Set of valid types."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_pom_errorprone_ProjectVersionAsDependencyVersion",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Project Version as Dependency Version",
    "description" : "This rule disallows using ${project.version} to specify dependency versions in Maven POM files.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.4\n\nUsing that expression in dependency declarations seems like a shortcut, but it can go wrong.\nBy far the most common problem is the use of ${project.version} in a BOM or parent POM.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<project...>\n  ...\n  <dependency>\n    ...\n    <version>${project.version}</version>\n  </dependency>\n</project>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_swift_bestpractices_ProhibitedInterfaceBuilder",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Creating Views Using Interface Builder",
    "description" : "This rule discourages creating views using Interface Builder and recommends building views programmatically.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nCreating views using Interface Builder should be avoided.\n            Defining views by code allows the compiler to detect issues that otherwise will be runtime errors.\n            It's difficult to review the auto-generated code and allow concurrent modifications of those files.\n            Consider building views programmatically.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nclass ViewController: UIViewController {\n    @IBOutlet var label: UILabel! // violation, referencing a IBOutlet\n}\n\nclass ViewController: UIViewController {\n    var label: UILabel!\n}\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Swift"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_swift_bestpractices_UnavailableFunction",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Mark Unimplemented Functions as Unavailable",
    "description" : "Unimplemented functions that are required but not needed should be marked as unavailable to prevent their execution while satisfying the compiler.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nDue to Objective-C and Swift interoperability some functions are often required to be implemented but\n            aren't really needed. It is extremely common that the sole implementation of the functions consist of throwing\n            a fatal error. Marking these functions as unavailable prevents them from being executed while still making\n            the compiler happy.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nrequired init?(coder _: NSCoder) { // violation, no unavailable attribute added to the function declaration\n    fatalError(\"init(coder:) has not been implemented\")\n}\n\n@available(*, unavailable)         // no violation\nrequired init?(coder _: NSCoder) {\n    fatalError(\"init(coder:) has not been implemented\")\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Swift"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_swift_errorprone_ForceCast",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Force Cast",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding force casting in Swift code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nForce casts should be avoided. This may lead to a crash if it's not used carefully.\n            For example assuming a JSON property has a given type, or your reused Cell has a certain contract.\n            Consider using conditional casting and handling the resulting optional.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nNSNumber() as! Int // violation, force casting\n\nNSNumber() as? Int // no violation\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Swift"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_swift_errorprone_ForceTry",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Force Try",
    "description" : "This rule enforces avoiding force try statements in Swift code.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nForce tries should be avoided. If the code being wrapped happens to raise and exception, our application will crash.\n            Consider using a conditional try and handling the resulting optional, or wrapping the try statement in a do-catch block.\n\nExample(s):\n```\nlet x = try! someThrowingFunction() // violation, force trying\n\nlet x = try? someThrowingFunction() // no violation\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Swift"
      }
    ]
  },
