    "id" : "category_visualforce_security_VfCsrf",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "CSRF",
    "title" : "Avoid Calling Visualforce Action Upon Page Load",
    "description" : "This rule detects when Visualforce pages call controller actions during page load, which can make the page vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid calling VF action upon page load as the action becomes vulnerable to CSRF.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<apex:page controller=\"AcRestActionsController\" action=\"{!csrfInitMethod}\" >\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "VisualForce"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_visualforce_security_VfHtmlStyleTagXss",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Enforce Correct Encoding for Expressions Within Style Tags in Visualforce",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that expressions within <style> tags in Visualforce pages use the correct encoding to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 6.31.0\n\nChecks for the correct encoding in `&lt;style/&gt;` tags in Visualforce pages.\n\nThe rule is based on Salesforce Security's recommendation to prevent XSS in Visualforce as mentioned\non [Secure Coding Cross Site Scripting](https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.secure_coding_guide.meta/secure_coding_guide/secure_coding_cross_site_scripting.htm).\n\nIn order to avoid cross site scripting, the relevant encoding must be used in HTML tags. The rule expects\n`URLENCODING` or `JSINHTMLENCODING` for URL-based style values and any kind of encoding\n(e.g. `HTMLENCODING`) for non-url style values.\n\nSee also {% rule &quot;VfUnescapeEl&quot; %} to check escaping in other places on Visualforce pages.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<apex:page>\n    <style>\n        div {\n            background: url('{!XSSHere}'); // Potential XSS\n        }\n        div {\n            background: url('{!URLENCODE(XSSHere)}'); // correct encoding\n        }\n    </style>\n</apex:page>\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "VisualForce"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_visualforce_security_VfUnescapeEl",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "XSS",
    "title" : "Avoid Unescaped User-Controlled Content in Visualforce EL",
    "description" : "This rule prevents the use of unescaped user-controlled content in Visualforce Expression Language (EL) to avoid cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.6.0\n\nAvoid unescaped user controlled content in EL as it results in XSS.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<apex:outputText value=\"Potential XSS is {! here }\" escape=\"false\" />\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "VisualForce"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_xml_bestpractices_MissingEncoding",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Explicit XML Encoding Declaration",
    "description" : "Ensure that an explicit character encoding is set in the XML declaration to guarantee proper parsing of the XML document.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 7.0.0\n\nWhen the character encoding is missing from the XML declaration,\nthe parser may produce garbled text.\n\nThis is completely dependent on how the parser is set up\nand the content of the XML file, so it may be hard to reproduce.\n\nProviding an explicit encoding ensures  accurate and consistent\nparsing.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n\n```",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_xml_errorprone_MistypedCDATASection",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Mistyped CDATA Section",
    "description" : "Detects CDATA sections in XML that have extra square brackets at the beginning or end, which may indicate a mistyped CDATA section.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAn XML CDATA section begins with a &lt;![CDATA[ marker, which has only one [, and ends with a ]]&gt; marker, which has two ].\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<root>\n    <child>\n        <![CDATA[[ character data ]]> - this cdata section is valid, but it contains an\n        additional square bracket at the beginning.\n        It should probably be just <![CDATA[ character data ]]>.\n    </child>\n    <child>\n        <![CDATA[ character data ]]]> - this cdata section is valid, but it contains an\n        additional square bracket in the end.\n        It should probably be just <![CDATA[ character data ]]>.\n    </child>\n</root>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_xsl_codestyle_UseConcatOnce",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use Concat Once in XPath Expressions",
    "description" : "Enforce using a single concat() function with multiple arguments instead of nested concat() calls in XPath expressions.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nThe XPath concat() functions accepts as many arguments as required so you can have\n&quot;concat($a,'b',$c)&quot; rather than &quot;concat($a,concat('b',$c)&quot;.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<xsl:variable name=\"var\" select=\"concat(\"Welcome\",concat(\"to you \",$name))\"/>\n<xsl:variable name=\"var\" select=\"concat(\"Welcome\",\"to you \",$name))\">\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "category_xsl_performance_AvoidAxisNavigation",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Axis Navigation in XPath Queries",
    "description" : "This rule flags the use of certain XPath axes such as 'following', 'preceding', 'descendant', or 'descendant-or-self' that can negatively impact performance by traversing large portions of the XML document.",
    "explanation" : "Since: PMD 5.0\n\nAvoid using the 'following' or 'preceding' axes whenever possible, as these can cut\nthrough 100% of the document in the worst case.  Also, try to avoid using 'descendant'\nor 'descendant-or-self' axes, as if you're at the top of the Document, it necessarily means\ncutting through 100% of the document.\n\nExample(s):\n```\n<xsl:variable name=\"var\" select=\"//item/descendant::child\"/>\n```",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "checkSelfDescendantAbreviation",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "descendant::self abreviation, '//', will also trigger this rule."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "XML"
      }
    ]
  }
]